.TH TEST 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
test, [ \- evaluate a conditional expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B test
.I expression
.br
.B [
.I expression
.B ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B test
evaluates
.I expression
and reports the result in its exit status: 0 if it is true, 1 if it is
false, and 2 if it is invalid.
It reads no input and writes nothing to standard output.
.PP
.B [
is the same command with a required last argument that is a lone
.BR ] ,
which is not part of the expression.
.PP
The kit's shell has both as builtins that run this same code, so
scripts see the behavior described here; see
.BR sh (1).
Outside the shell, the
.I test.exe
launcher runs the
.B busybox
applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
There is no launcher for
.BR [ ,
which is run as
.BR "busybox [" .
Unlike bash and stock busybox-w32, the kit's shell has no
.B [[
command; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
.B test
has no options.
Arguments such as
.B \-\-help
are ordinary words of the expression, so
.B test \-\-help
is true because its one argument is not empty.
.PP
Each operator, operand, and parenthesis is a separate argument.
In the shell, quote or escape the characters it treats specially, such
as
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR < ,
and
.BR > .
Strings are compared as bytes.
.SS Number of arguments
An expression of up to four arguments is first resolved by counting
them, mostly as POSIX specifies.
So an operand that looks like an operator is safe:
.B [ \-f = \-f ]
compares two strings, and
.B [ ! = ! ]
is true.
.TP
.B 0
False.
.TP
.B 1
True if the argument is not empty, whatever it is, so
.B [ \-f ]
and
.B [ ! ]
are true.
.TP
.B 2
If the first argument is
.BR ! ,
true if the second is empty.
Otherwise the first must be a unary operator and the second is its
operand, or the expression is invalid.
.TP
.B 3
If the second argument is a binary operator other than
.B \-a
or
.BR \-o ,
the result of that operator applied to the first and third, even when
the first is
.B !\&
or
.BR ( .
Otherwise, if the first is
.BR ! ,
the negation of the two-argument expression of the rest.
.TP
.B 4
If the first argument is
.BR ! ,
the negation of the three-argument expression of the rest, except that
when the third is
.B \-a
or
.BR \-o ,
the
.B !\&
applies to the second alone, where POSIX negates the whole.
.PP
Any other expression uses the full grammar under
.BR "Combining expressions" ,
apart from a quirk with parentheses described in
.BR NOTES .
.SS File tests
These take a
.I file
operand.
Every test except
.B \-L
and
.B \-h
follows symbolic links and applies to the target.
A file that cannot be examined for any reason, such as a missing path or
denied access, is treated as nonexistent, and its tests are false.
A name that ends in a directory separator can only be a directory.
.TP
.BI "\-b " file
True if
.I file
is a block special file.
Always false, since there are none.
.TP
.BI "\-c " file
True if
.I file
is a character special file.
These are the emulated devices
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/tty ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
and
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.I /dev/fd/N
for each open file descriptor number
.IR N ,
except that the last four are named pipes when their descriptor is
open on a pipe.
.TP
.BI "\-d " file
True if
.I file
is a directory.
.TP
.BI "\-e " file
True if
.I file
exists.
.TP
.BI "\-f " file
True if
.I file
is a regular file: it exists and is neither a directory nor one of the
emulated devices.
.TP
.BI "\-G " file
True if
.I file
exists and its group is the user's group.
busybox gives a file the same ID for its owner and its group, so this is
the same as
.BR \-O .
.TP
.BI "\-g " file
True if
.I file
has its set-group-ID bit set.
Always false.
.TP
.BI "\-k " file
True if
.I file
has its sticky bit set.
Always false.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-h\fR \fIfile\fR
True if
.I file
is a symbolic link, a directory junction or volume mount point, or an
app execution alias.
It does not follow the link.
.TP
.BI "\-O " file
True if
.I file
exists and is owned by the effective user.
See
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BI "\-p " file
True if
.I file
is a named pipe.
Only
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.I /dev/fd/N
can be, when their descriptor is open on a pipe, so
.B "test \-p /dev/stdin"
tells whether standard input is a pipe.
.TP
.BI "\-r " file
True if
.I file
exists and is readable.
See
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BI "\-S " file
True if
.I file
is a socket.
Always false.
.TP
.BI "\-s " file
True if
.I file
exists and its size is not zero.
A directory has size zero.
.TP
.BI "\-t " fd
True if file descriptor
.I fd
is open on a console.
A pipe, a file, and the NUL device are not consoles.
The argument must be a decimal number.
.TP
.BI "\-u " file
True if
.I file
has its set-user-ID bit set.
Always false.
.TP
.BI "\-w " file
True if
.I file
exists and is writable.
See
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BI "\-x " file
True if
.I file
exists and is executable.
See
.BR NOTES .
.SS String tests
.TP
.BI "\-n " string
True if
.I string
is not empty.
.TP
.BI "\-z " string
True if
.I string
is empty.
.TP
.I string
True if
.I string
is not empty.
This is the one-argument form, and the same as
.BR \-n .
.TP
\fIstring1\fR \fB=\fR \fIstring2\fR, \fIstring1\fR \fB==\fR \fIstring2\fR
True if the strings are equal.
.TP
\fIstring1\fR \fB!=\fR \fIstring2\fR
True if the strings are not equal.
.TP
\fIstring1\fR \fB<\fR \fIstring2\fR
True if
.I string1
sorts before
.IR string2 ,
comparing bytes.
.TP
\fIstring1\fR \fB>\fR \fIstring2\fR
True if
.I string1
sorts after
.IR string2 ,
comparing bytes.
.SS Integer comparisons
Each operand is a decimal integer, with optional leading white space, an
optional sign, and optional trailing white space.
It is a signed 64-bit value.
An operand that is not a number, such as an empty string or one in
hexadecimal, is the error
.BR "bad number" ,
and one that does not fit is the error
.BR "out of range" ;
both end the command with status 2.
.TP
\fIn1\fR \fB\-eq\fR \fIn2\fR
True if the numbers are equal.
.TP
\fIn1\fR \fB\-ne\fR \fIn2\fR
True if they are not equal.
.TP
\fIn1\fR \fB\-ge\fR \fIn2\fR
True if
.I n1
is greater than or equal to
.IR n2 .
.TP
\fIn1\fR \fB\-gt\fR \fIn2\fR
True if
.I n1
is greater than
.IR n2 .
.TP
\fIn1\fR \fB\-le\fR \fIn2\fR
True if
.I n1
is less than or equal to
.IR n2 .
.TP
\fIn1\fR \fB\-lt\fR \fIn2\fR
True if
.I n1
is less than
.IR n2 .
.SS File comparisons
.TP
\fIfile1\fR \fB\-ef\fR \fIfile2\fR
True if both files exist and are the same file: the same volume and
file index.
See
.BR NOTES .
.TP
\fIfile1\fR \fB\-nt\fR \fIfile2\fR
True if
.I file1
exists and either
.I file2
does not or
.I file1
was modified later than
.IR file2 ,
comparing times in whole seconds.
.TP
\fIfile1\fR \fB\-ot\fR \fIfile2\fR
True if
.I file2
exists and either
.I file1
does not or
.I file1
was modified earlier than
.IR file2 ,
comparing times in whole seconds.
.SS Combining expressions
From highest precedence to lowest:
.TP
\fB(\fR \fIexpression\fR \fB)\fR
True if
.I expression
is true.
Parentheses group.
.TP
\fB!\fR \fIexpression\fR
True if
.I expression
is false.
.TP
\fIexpression1\fR \fB\-a\fR \fIexpression2\fR
True if both are true.
.TP
\fIexpression1\fR \fB\-o\fR \fIexpression2\fR
True if either is true.
.PP
Both operands of
.B \-a
and
.B \-o
are always evaluated, so an error in the right one ends the command with
status 2 even when the left one decides the result.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands wildcards, even in quoted arguments, when
.I test.exe
starts, which serves commands typed in
.BR cmd.exe .
So
.B "test.exe \-f \(dq*.c\(dq"
typed there fails with an unknown operand error when more than one file
matches.
Set it to
.B 0
to prevent that.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal, by default
.BR 002 .
Without its
.B 002
bit, a file owned by someone else is writable unless it is read-only;
see
.BR NOTES .
The shell's
.B umask
command sets it.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The expression is true.
.TP
.B 1
The expression is false, or there are no arguments.
.TP
.B 2
The expression is invalid.
A message on standard error says why:
.BR "argument expected" ,
.BR "bad number" ,
.BR "closing paren expected" ,
.BR "missing ]" ,
.BR "out of range" ,
or
.BR "unknown operand" ,
usually after the argument at fault.
It begins with the command name, or in the shell with the shell's name,
such as
.BR sh .
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a command only if a file exists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ [ \-f config.h ] && echo found
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create a directory unless one that can be written already exists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ [ \-d build \-a \-w build ] || mkdir build
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test a variable for emptiness.
Quote it: unquoted, an empty value leaves the single argument
.BR \-n ,
which is true:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ x=
$ [ \-n $x ] && echo unquoted is true
unquoted is true
$ [ \-n "$x" ] || echo quoted is false
quoted is false
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compare numbers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ [ "$n" \-ge 10 ] && echo at least ten
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rebuild unless the object file exists and is newer than the source:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ [ main.o \-nt main.c ] || gcc \-c main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B test
cannot match a string against a pattern.
Use the shell's
.B case
command instead:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ name=main.c
$ case $name in *.c) echo C source;; esac
C source
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.B test
and bash:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-nt
and
.B \-ot
compare whole seconds.
.IP \(bu 2
The bash unary operators
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-N ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-R ,
and
.B \-v
are not supported.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B [[
command, so bash's pattern and regular expression tests are not
available.
busybox-w32 builds
.B [[
into its shell for bash compatibility, and w64devkit leaves it out.
To match a pattern, use the shell's
.B case
command.
.PP
File names follow Windows rules.
Either slash separates components, drive letters work, and a leading
slash is the root of the current drive.
MSYS-style names such as
.I /c/Users
are not translated.
The device names
.B NUL
and
.B CON
do not exist: use
.IR /dev/null .
The
.I /dev
names under
.B \-c
are recognized by spelling, not by looking at the file system, so
they work from any directory and drive but
.I C:/dev/null
does not.
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Permissions come from a Unix-style mode that busybox builds for each file
from its attributes and name, and tests against the effective user ID.
Any file is readable.
A directory is writable, and so is a regular file without the read-only
attribute.
A directory is executable, and so is a regular file whose name ends in
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.B .cmd
in any case, or whose contents begin with
.B #!\&
or are a PE executable that is not a DLL.
For other names, except
.BR .dll ,
that check reads the start of the file, unless its data is not stored
locally.
A file owned by someone else is writable only if, in addition, its
access control list grants the user full access or the umask lacks the
bit
.B 002
that removes write permission for others; see
.BR BB_UMASK .
A file whose security information cannot be opened is none of readable,
writable, or executable.
.PP
An elevated administrator process has user ID 0, as
.BR id (1)
shows, and then, as root would on Unix,
.B \-r
and
.B \-w
are true for every file that can be examined, and
.B \-x
for every executable one.
The user ID is otherwise 4095.
.B \-O
compares the effective user ID with the owner found in the file's
security information, which busybox maps to a number: 4095 for the
current user, the relative ID of another local or domain account if
that is from 500 to 4094, and 0 for any other owner, such as SYSTEM or
the Administrators group, or when the information cannot be read.
An emulated device, or a file whose data is not stored locally, belongs
to the current user.
So an elevated process owns the files that map to 0, and not those owned
by its own account.
.PP
.B \-ef
compares the volume serial number and file index, so hard links, and a
link or junction and its target, are the same file.
The emulated devices, files whose data is not stored locally, and any
other files whose identity cannot be read all compare equal to each
other.
.PP
When the whole expression, after any leading
.BR ! ,
is a unary test in parentheses, such as
.B ( \-n x )
or
.BR "! ( \-d dir )" ,
it fails with
.B closing paren expected
and status 2.
So does a unary operator or
.B !\&
alone in parentheses, such as
.BR "( \-n )" ,
which POSIX makes a test of a non-empty string.
Leave out the parentheses.
A longer expression, such as
.B ( ! \-d dir )
or
.BR "( \-n x ) \-a y" ,
is not affected.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR expr (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR sh (1)
